{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>For every matter of trespass.</b> That a liar be found in his oath — that witnesses testify that he himself stole it — and Elohim convict him by the mouth of the witnesses." "<b>He shall pay double to his fellow.</b> Scripture teaches you that one who claims regarding a deposit, saying 'it was stolen from it,' and it turns out that he himself stole it — pays double payment. And when? When he swore and afterward witnesses came. For thus our Sages expounded: 'And the master of the house shall draw near to Elohim' — this drawing near is an oath. Do you say an oath, or is it not rather to judgment? For since he came to judgment and denied, saying 'it was stolen' — would he immediately be liable for double if witnesses came that it is in his hand? 'Sending forth of the hand' is stated here and 'sending forth of the hand' is stated below — ״an oath of YHWH shall be between them both, if he has not sent forth his hand״ (ibid.) — just as there it is an oath, so too here it is an oath (ibid.)." "<b>Whom he declares: That is it.</b> According to its plain sense: whom the witness declares — 'it is that very one about which you swore; it is in your possession' — the matter of both of them shall come before the judges, and they shall investigate the witnesses. If they are valid and they convict this bailee, he shall pay double; and if they convict the witnesses, who are found to be conspiring, they shall pay double to the bailee. And our Sages of blessed memory expounded 'כִּי הוּא זֶה' to teach that they do not obligate him in an oath unless he admitted partially, saying 'I owe you such-and-such, and the rest was stolen from me' (ibid. 107)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:54.066607571Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}